· python3 - <<'PY' from pathlib import Path readme = Path("README.md") text = readme.read_text() section = '''## The durations package `parse_duration(text: str) -> int` turns a duration into seconds. Plain durations are one or more `<number><unit>` parts, in any order, with the units `s`, `m`, `h` and `d`; case and surrounding spaces are ignored. ```python >>> from durations import parse_duration >>> parse_duration("1h30m") 5400 ``` ISO 8601 durations such as `PT1H30M` or `P2DT4H` work too: they start with `P`, their parts are in order (`PnYnMnDTnHnMnS`, or weeks alone as `PnW`), a year counts as 365 days and a month as 30 days, and anything else the parser cannot read raises `ValueError`. ```python >>> parse_duration("P2DT4H") 187200 ``` The same parser runs from the command line: each duration prints on its own line, and input that cannot be parsed exits with status 2. ```bash $ python3 -m durations 1h30m P2DT4H 5400 187200 ``` ''' assert text.count("## Recorded runs") == 1 readme.write_text(text.replace("## Recorded runs", section + "## Recorded runs", 1)) PY git diff README.md
